"""Review exercises that apply each Understandable criterion to a new case."""

REVIEW = {
'3.1.1': 'Review case: A university publishes an English application form and a separate Japanese translation. Both are generated from the same template, which hard-codes lang="en". A screenshot of the Japanese version looks correct, yet the inherited language metadata remains English. Change the template to receive the page language from the translation data, and verify that it sets the root attribute on a direct load as well as when navigating from English. Inspect any application shell that stays in English separately: the language of a part may need different markup. Do not derive the document language only from the user’s browser preferences, since those preferences can differ from the content actually displayed. Compare the accessible language exposed for the root with the visible page content and record the route used to reach the page. This evidence distinguishes an incorrectly configured translation from a missing speech voice.',
'3.1.2': 'Review case: A course in English contains a German quotation followed by an English explanation. The author puts lang="de" on a container that wraps both paragraphs. A screen reader that uses language changes can apply German pronunciation to the English explanation as well. Move the language attribute onto the quotation itself, inspect the resulting nesting, and verify that the English explanation again inherits the page default. A visual heading such as “German quotation” does not itself expose the language of the quoted words. If the quotation contains a proper name, assess the criterion’s exception in context rather than indiscriminately marking individual words. Test the boundary after content has been saved through the editor: exported markup can differ from the initial source. The goal is that software can determine the language of the relevant passage, not that every reader must use a particular voice.',
'3.1.3': 'Review case: A tutorial tells a student to “roll back the migration” but offers only a dictionary definition of roll as movement. In this context, a rollback means reversing a database change, and the exact effect depends on the migration. Explain the intended technical sense, then state which operation the student should perform and what result to expect. If a glossary provides the definition, make the link meaningful and reachable without hover; place enough context in the surrounding instructions that a learner can decide when to consult it. Test the wording with someone who has not previously worked with migrations. Ask them to distinguish rolling back a change from deleting the entire database. Revise the entry if it is technically misleading or only substitutes more specialized vocabulary. A definition mechanism must supply the intended meaning, not simply a list of possible unrelated senses.',
'3.1.4': 'Review case: An accessibility report uses “AT” to mean assistive technology, but a separate networking page uses the same letters for a different term. A site-wide expansion chosen without context may mislead readers. Expand the term where it is introduced in each relevant document, or provide a glossary that clearly distinguishes the meanings. Verify that a learner entering through a search result can identify the expansion without first finding the home page. If an abbr element has a title attribute, test how the expansion is encountered with keyboard, touch, and a screen reader; visible text or a reachable explanation avoids depending entirely on a hover tooltip. Expansion and comprehension are related but distinct: spelling out “assistive technology” may still require a short explanation that it refers to tools used to interact with content.',
'3.1.5': 'Review case: A policy states that applications received after a certain date may be considered only if the department approves an exception. A simplified version that says “Late applications are accepted” changes the policy. Preserve the condition and explain who makes the decision, what the applicant should submit, and how the decision will be communicated when those details are available. If the original is uncertain or incomplete, do not invent a promise in the alternative. Ask a new learner to identify the standard deadline and describe the exception using both versions. Check whether a diagram or example includes the same condition, and provide its essential information in text. A readability formula can highlight complexity but cannot verify that the policy’s conditions and consequences remain accurate. Maintain the supplemental version when the original changes.',
'3.1.6': 'Review case: A vocabulary exercise presents the isolated word “lead” and asks which meaning it has. Without more context, a reader may need to know whether the intended pronunciation denotes a metal or the action of guiding. Supply a context-specific pronunciation or meaning mechanism beside that occurrence. A written stress or sound guide may be easier for some readers to use than a recording, while a recording may help others; choose an explanation suited to the audience and make any control clearly labeled. Test the guidance with a learner who has not heard the instructor’s spoken explanation. If the sentence already unambiguously establishes the intended sense, assess whether this criterion’s condition is met rather than mechanically adding the same pronunciation widget everywhere. Confirm the guide remains accurate when the example is translated.',
'3.2.1': 'Review case: A search result opens its detail page as soon as the result receives focus. This may appear convenient when using a mouse, but a keyboard user cannot pass through the result list to explore other choices. Replace the focus-triggered navigation with a standard link whose destination opens on activation. Starting before the list, tab through all results and back with Shift+Tab. Record whether the URL, active window, or focus location changes before an explicit activation. Then activate one link and verify that it reaches the correct page. Also inspect event handlers added by third-party components; disabling a handler in the visible template will not fix the interaction if another listener still redirects. Showing a focus outline or a small explanatory hint is different from moving the learner to a new context.',
'3.2.2': 'Review case: A region selector updates the selected region locally and also immediately redirects to a new page when the value changes. A keyboard user may select an intermediate option accidentally while moving through the list. Provide an Apply button or give clear advance information when immediate navigation is intended. Verify the browser location does not change while a user explores values in the button-based design, and check that activating Apply opens the chosen region rather than a previously selected value. Do not classify every dynamic calculation as a context change: updating a displayed shipping estimate without redirecting or moving focus has different effects, though its important updates must still be communicated accessibly. Preserve unsaved entries when the application transitions, so the learner does not unexpectedly lose work.',
'3.2.3': 'Review case: On one page, a repeated navigation sequence is Home, Courses, Help; on another, it becomes Home, Help, Courses because a mobile-specific component has a different link array. A learner who has memorized the sequence must relearn it on the second page. Consolidate the repeated links or otherwise synchronize their relative order, then compare the two pages at both wide and narrow widths. An additional page-specific item does not necessarily violate the requirement if the repeated items keep their relative positions. Check the order encountered with keyboard and screen reader, since CSS can visually rearrange elements independently of the source. If the learner explicitly reorders a personalized menu, document that user-initiated choice separately from an unexpected site-wide inconsistency.',
'3.2.4': 'Review case: A calendar icon opens a date picker on several forms, but one instance is announced as “Calendar,” another as “Choose date,” and a third has no accessible name. Inspect both what each button does and how it is identified. Use consistent, meaningful identification for the repeated date-selection function; preserve a visible label relationship where applicable. Then check a calendar icon that opens a schedule rather than a picker: because its function is different, an identical label could itself mislead. A consistent icon is helpful visual recognition, but it does not supply an accessible name on its own. Verify each instance in the accessibility tree, activate it, and check the result. This distinguishes consistent identification from merely giving every control with a similar picture the same text.',
'3.2.5': 'Review case: A lesson displays a transcript and automatically moves to the next page five seconds after the video finishes. A learner who reads more slowly may lose the transcript before finishing. Provide an explicit Next control, or a mechanism to turn off the automatic change, and make that control available before the transition. Test whether the setting remains effective when the video ends and whether the user can advance manually afterward. A progress indicator that updates in place does not necessarily change context; the audit should observe whether the page, focus, or another significant context actually changes. Also inspect transitions from timers and background responses, not only those caused by focus or a changed form control. The question is whether the transition is user-initiated or an accessible turn-off mechanism exists.',
'3.2.6': 'Review case: A site includes both a contact link and an automated support chat on its course, lesson, and assessment pages. On the assessment page the chat appears before the contact link, while on the other pages the order is reversed. Compare the repeated mechanisms in relation to each other and surrounding page elements, including in the mobile menu. Preserve a consistent relative sequence unless the user has chosen to change it. Test that the controls themselves work, but distinguish operational defects from this particular ordering requirement. A page that never offers a certain help mechanism does not automatically fail merely because the criterion mentions that kind of help; the requirement concerns relevant mechanisms that repeat within a set. Keep an audit record showing pages, viewport, and the order found.',
'3.3.1': 'Review case: A form accepts an email address locally but the server rejects it because it is already registered. Display a text message that identifies the affected email field and describes the problem, provided the disclosure is appropriate for the application’s security context. Associate the message with the field and preserve the rest of the learner’s input. If a summary appears, make it possible to reach the problematic field and ensure the same message remains available there. A color change or an icon can reinforce the error but cannot replace its textual identification. Test after correcting the value: remove or update the obsolete message, and confirm a screen reader user can find the current state. In a security-sensitive flow, choose wording that identifies the problem without unnecessarily revealing account information.',
'3.3.2': 'Review case: A date field has the placeholder “MM/DD/YYYY” but no visible label. Once a learner starts typing, the placeholder disappears, leaving no persistent indication of what the date represents or what format is expected. Add a visible label identifying the date and separate instructions describing the format accepted by the application. Associate both with the control programmatically, and communicate if the value is required. If the product accepts multiple formats, do not falsely state that only one is permitted. Test a screen reader’s announced name and description, then enter a value to check that the guidance remains available. A fieldset and legend are useful when several controls answer one shared question, but individual controls still need understandable labels.',
'3.3.3': 'Review case: A registration form says “Password invalid” although it knows that the password must contain at least twelve characters. When disclosure is safe, explain the requirement and retain the user’s input so it can be corrected. Test too-short input and a valid input, and ensure the guidance agrees with server-side validation. Do not expose a user’s secret in an error summary or provide suggestions that compromise security. A general sign-in error may be appropriate when specifying whether the account or password was wrong would reveal sensitive information. Separate the question “Which item has an error?” from “What known change would fix it?”; both may need to be addressed. Check that the learner can find the relevant message when returning to the field.',
'3.3.4': 'Review case: A learner submits an assessment whose response is final. Before confirmation, show the answers and allow the learner to return to an item and change it. Deliberately change one response during review and verify the final submitted answer reflects that change. If the application claims reversibility instead, test whether a submitted response can actually be withdrawn or corrected; a visual Undo control that leaves the stored result unchanged is not an effective safeguard. If relying on checking, identify which errors the system detects and confirm a user can correct them. The requirement covers specified high-consequence categories, so determine whether the interaction falls within its scope and record the safeguard used rather than assuming every form must have a review page.',
'3.3.5': 'Review case: A form asks for a student identifier but learners do not know where to find it. Provide field-specific help such as the location on an enrollment notice and an example that matches the expected format, without displaying real personal identifiers. Place a concise explanation near the field and link to fuller guidance if necessary. Test opening that guidance with the keyboard, reading it at increased zoom, and returning without losing the value already entered. If a dialog is used, check focus entry, an accessible close control, and a sensible return point. Verify examples against validation rules when the identifier format changes. Context-sensitive help should answer the problem at this step; sending users to an unrelated general support page can leave the key question unanswered.',
'3.3.6': 'Review case: A learner submits a profile biography and later notices an accidental edit. If the product offers Undo as its safeguard, save a change, invoke Undo, refresh the page, and verify the previously stored biography is restored. If the product uses a review-and-confirm path instead, edit the biography from review and verify that the final record contains the corrected text. If relying on checking, demonstrate a detectable error and an opportunity to fix it. The user should receive clear feedback when an operation succeeds or fails. This enhanced criterion applies to user submissions more broadly than the high-consequence situations of the related minimum criterion, but the chosen safeguard must still provide a practical correction opportunity rather than a decorative confirmation.',
'3.3.7': 'Review case: A learner enters an email address at the start of an application and is asked for the same address again during a later step. Offer the existing address for selection or populate it, then allow an alternative address if the task requires one. Verify the final application contains the learner’s selected value and that navigating backward does not unexpectedly replace it. Consider whether the request is within the same process and whether previous information remains valid. The criterion provides exceptions for essential re-entry, security, and information that is no longer valid; evaluate the actual reason for this field instead of applying an unsupported blanket exception. Showing the reused value helps a learner catch a typo, but avoid exposing sensitive information to an unintended person.',
'3.3.8': 'Review case: A one-time code arrives by email and the form divides it across six inputs. If a learner pastes the complete code, the application should support a practical assisting mechanism rather than forcing the person to remember and transcribe one digit at a time. Test paste and the device’s available autofill in the real form; inspect whether focus movement, hidden fields, or input validation disrupts the operation. Also test the required recovery path and any second factor. An alternative is not useful if the learner must first solve the inaccessible challenge to reach it. The minimum criterion includes specified exceptions for object recognition and personal content, but their presence does not establish that a visual puzzle is broadly usable.',
'3.3.9': 'Review case: A service permits password-manager fill on its normal login screen but requires a personal-photo challenge for recovery. Assess recovery separately: the enhanced criterion does not carry over the minimum criterion’s personal-content and object-recognition exceptions. Where that challenge is a cognitive function test, the learner needs an alternative without the test or an assisting mechanism. Test the alternate path from the point where recovery begins, including any required second factor and enrollment step. A passkey can reduce memorization, but verify the user can access a suitable device and has a usable recovery route if the device is unavailable. Document the complete path and observed assistance rather than declaring conformance based on a single button.'
}
